We probably have see this error before, let us have a concrete solution for calico:



Back-off restarting failed container install-cni in pod calico-node-2x7hh_kube-system(d7f36227-371e-42d9-a619-1237fba45e33)

We are right back to the original error we started with on the install-cni init container (which must complete successfully before the main calico-node container can start).
However, there is a massive clue here: this is a new pod (calico-node-2x7hh) spawned during our recent updates. Because the control plane (kube-apiserver) is now stable, the root cause is no longer a database timeout. Instead, the init container is crashing because of the Token Creation RBAC issue we uncovered earlier.
------------------------------
## Why this is happening right now
When you edited the DaemonSet to clean up the BGP flags, Kubernetes destroyed the old pods and created fresh ones (like 2x7hh).
During its installation phase, Calico tries to use the Kubernetes TokenRequest API to provision short-lived security tokens for the CNI plugin. If the calico-node cluster role lacks explicit permission to generate these tokens, the init container crashes instantly, throwing your Back-off restarting failed container install-cni error.
------------------------------
## The Fix: Apply the Missing RBAC Permissions
You can fix this globally in under 60 seconds by granting the missing token permission to the Calico cluster role.
## Step 1: Open the ClusterRole for Editing
Run this command from your Windows workstation terminal:

kubectl edit clusterrole calico-node --kubeconfig=.kube/config

## Step 2: Append the Token Request Rules
Scroll to the very bottom of the rules: array and add this block exactly as written:

- apiGroups: [""]
  resources:
    - serviceaccounts/token
  resourceNames:
    - calico-cni-plugin
  verbs:
    - create

Note: Ensure your spacing matches the existing blocks perfectly (Kubernetes uses 2-space indentation).
## Step 3: Save and Force an Immediate Cycle

   1. Save and close your text editor (:wq in vim).
   2. Delete the stuck pod to force it to retry with its new security clearance immediately:
   
   kubectl delete pod -n kube-system calico-node-2x7hh --kubeconfig=.kube/config
   
   
## What to Expect Next
The fresh init container will boot up, successfully generate its security tokens, write the CNI binaries, exit with a clean 0 code, and hand over execution to your newly optimized IPAM-only calico-node container.
Once you apply the ClusterRole token update, does the install-cni init container successfully finish its job and pass execution forward to a healthy 1/1 READY status?

